 "name": "Reflective Facades",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the online survey on two reflective facades in Leicester, UK, with the paper's own photographs: the Highcross shopping centre, clad in buckled reflective stainless-steel panels, and the Mattioli Woods PLC office building, with a fully glazed reflective curtain wall. For each building, participants see the paper's two photos and rate seven statements on the paper's 1-5 scale (1 = strongly disagree, 3 = neutral, 5 = strongly agree): visual discomfort from brightness, glare or shine; visual interest or attractiveness; disorientation or confusion; influence on the perception of the surroundings; stress; feeling tense or uneasy; and effect on mood or focus. They then say which facade felt more visually comfortable and which caused more discomfort or eye strain, and may add a comment. Rebuilt from Joslyn & Abdelwahab (2025), Reflective facades' impacts on visual perception and psychological responses, Architecture 5(3): 66, Section 3.2, with the photos from its Figure 1 (CC BY 4.0, shown as printed) and the statement wording from the titles of its Figures 2 and 3. Answers: each statement is keyed by building (highcross or mattioli), e.g. discomfort keyed highcross; shown lists that building's two photos; more_comfortable and more_strain hold the building chosen. Analysis as in the paper: medians and boxplots per statement and building, and a one-way ANOVA comparing the two facades for each statement. The paper (30 Leicester residents and students) found both buildings attractive (median 4); the metal facade caused more disorientation or confusion than the glass one (median 3 against 1, F(1, 58) = 11.86, p = 0.001) and slightly more visual discomfort (median 2 against 1, p = 0.074); stress and tension were low for both. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper does not print its demographic questions or the wording of its two comparison questions, so gender, whether the participant lives in Leicester, and the two comparison questions are worded by us; (2) the paper does not say which building came first, so the order of the two buildings is random; (3) the paper's survey ran on Microsoft Forms, and the introduction and consent text are ours.",
